WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Browsers manage cookies and may attach them to requests according to cookie rules.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:12.120
That behavior is useful because users do not need to manually present a session credential on every page.

3
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:21.180
It is also security-sensitive because a request can arrive with valid session cookies even when the server still needs to confirm the request belongs to the expected workflow.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.700
Server-side applications should treat state-changing browser requests differently from ordinary reads.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:40.280
A read request may display data, while a state-changing request can update a profile, submit a form, approve a workflow, create an export, delete a record, or change configuration.

6
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.060
The server should know which requests are meant to be safe reads and which requests modify state.

7
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.100
GET-style requests should not be used for state-changing actions.

8
00:00:50.200 --> 00:01:00.360
A GET request is expected to be safe to retrieve, bookmark, preview, cache, crawl, or repeat without changing application state.

9
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:07.340
When a GET request changes data, the application becomes harder to reason about and harder to protect consistently.

10
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.560
Use methods and endpoint design that clearly distinguish reads from changes.

11
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:20.600
Authentication identifies the user, but it does not prove that the user intentionally initiated a specific state-changing request.

12
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:29.980
Authorization decides whether the user can perform an action, but it does not prove that this exact browser request came from the intended application form or client flow.

13
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.080
That distinction is central to CSRF prevention.

14
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.640
CSRF controls add a request-intent signal.

15
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:45.420
In ASP.NET Core, antiforgery validation is the common way to protect browser-based state-changing requests.

16
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:53.920
The application expects a valid token along with the request and treats token validation as separate from identity and permission checks.

17
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.440
A practical review should classify endpoints by behavior.

18
00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:09.980
Which endpoints read data, which modify data, which use cookies, which are called by server-rendered forms, which are called by JavaScript clients, and which are APIs for non-browser clients.

19
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.200
The correct CSRF model depends on those answers.
